#module "fs"
#import "std/outcome"
using outcome
'' std/fs: fserror -- fs.FsError, and the Result convention for the filesystem
''
'' Specified in C:\dev\docs\filesystem\RFC-0001-errors.md.
''
'' Every filesystem operation that can fail returns Result( of T, FsError ) and
'' never sets err(), never raises, and never returns a magic value. Every
'' operation that CANNOT fail returns its value directly. The split is visible
'' in the signature, which is the whole point.
''
'' WHY Result AND NOT A SENTINEL. The datetime module uses TryParse plus an
'' Invalid sentinel, and for a date that is right -- "why not" is rarely
'' actionable. Filesystem calls are the opposite: the difference between NO SUCH
'' FILE, ACCESS DENIED, IS A DIRECTORY and DISK FULL changes what the program
'' does next -- create it, prompt for elevation, report a bug, or fail loudly.
'' That is exactly what Result carries and Optional does not (std/outcome).
''
'' THERE IS NO GENERIC TYPEDEF IN THE LANGUAGE. The obvious spelling
''
''     type FsResult( of T ) as Result( of T, FsError )
''
'' does not compile -- fbc reads `type X( of T )` as the opening of a generic
'' TYPE BODY and swallows the following statement:
''
''     error 338: Unbalanced block in generic body, FSRESULT
''
'' So the aliases below are concrete, one per T the module actually returns. The
'' set is small and closed, which is why this costs a table and not a
'' workaround. A `#define FsResult(T)` macro works and was REJECTED: macros
'' ignore NAMESPACE entirely, so as a header it would have injected an
'' unqualified FsResult into every translation unit that included it -- exactly
'' what inc/containers.bi:22 promised the library would not do.
''
'' IGNORING A RESULT STAYS A CONVENTION. No '?' operator, no must-use
'' diagnostic. What the type buys is that the failure has a name and a shape,
'' not that anyone is forced to look at it (std/outcome's result.tiko).



#if __FB_LANG__ <> "fb"
    #error "std/fs requires -lang fb (it uses namespaces)"
#endif

'' Same calling-convention switch as std/strings' FBSTR_EXTERN, for the same measured
'' reason: FBCALL is __stdcall on win32 and nothing everywhere else, and a bare
'' `extern "C"` inside a NAMESPACE asks the linker for an undecorated symbol
'' that win32 does not export. win64 cannot tell the two apart, so this is a
'' failure only a 32-bit build would ever show.
#if defined( __FB_WIN32__ ) and ( not defined( __FB_64BIT__ ) )
    #define FBFS_EXTERN extern "Windows"
#else
    #define FBFS_EXTERN extern "C"
#endif


'' A closed, portable enum: the codes a program can plausibly BRANCH on.
'' Everything else is FS_UNKNOWN plus a preserved native code.
''
'' These values must equal the FB_FS_* constants in tiko-rt's
'' lib/rt/src/inc/fserror.ti. fbc_tests.filesystem.error cross-checks every one
'' of them through fb_UStrFsErrorMessage, whose table is indexed by the C
'' constant -- so a divergence shows up as a wrong message, not as silence.
public enum FsErrorCode
    FS_OK = 0
    FS_NOT_FOUND            '' no such file or directory
    FS_ACCESS_DENIED        '' permission, or in use by policy
    FS_ALREADY_EXISTS       '' create refused because the target is there
    FS_NOT_A_DIRECTORY      '' a component that must be a directory is not
    FS_IS_A_DIRECTORY       '' a directory where a file is required
    FS_DIR_NOT_EMPTY        '' RemoveDir on a populated directory
    FS_INVALID_PATH         '' malformed, or a character the OS refuses
    FS_NAME_TOO_LONG        '' component or whole path exceeds the OS limit
    FS_LOOP                 '' too many symbolic links resolving one path
    FS_NO_SPACE             '' the volume is full, or a quota is exhausted
    FS_READ_ONLY            '' the volume or the object is write-protected
    FS_IN_USE               '' a sharing violation, or the file is locked
    FS_CROSS_DEVICE         '' rename across volumes
    FS_INTERRUPTED          '' a signal interrupted the call (POSIX EINTR)
    FS_UNSUPPORTED          '' the platform or filesystem cannot do this
    FS_UNKNOWN              '' mapped to nothing; OsCode( ) is the real answer

    '' APPENDED AFTER FS_UNKNOWN (C:\dev\docs\tiko-fs\RFC-0001 section 4), so every
    '' value above keeps the number a program may have stored.
    FS_CLOSED               '' an operation on a file that is not open
    FS_UNEXPECTED_EOF       '' the data ended before the amount asked for
    FS_NOT_SEEKABLE         '' a seek or positional IO on a pipe, socket or terminal
    FS_INVALID_DATA         '' text decoding met data it was told to reject
    FS_TOO_LARGE            '' a size limit was exceeded
    FS_WOULD_BLOCK          '' a non-blocking attempt found the lock held
    FS_TOO_MANY_LINKS       '' the hard-link limit was reached
    FS_BROKEN_PIPE          '' a write to a pipe with no reader
    FS_INVALID_ARGUMENT     '' contradictory options, a negative offset, a zero size

    '' APPENDED FOR SOCKETS (C:\dev\docs\tiko-stdlib\RFC-0001 D2): std/net's TcpStream
    '' reports through fs.Stream.  Until tiko-rt's table knows them, FsErrorMessage says
    '' "unknown error" for these two and FsError.Message( ) supplies the text itself.
    FS_TIMED_OUT            '' a read or write timeout ran out
    FS_CONNECTION_RESET     '' the peer reset the connection
end enum

FBFS_EXTERN
    '' Fixed English text for a portable code. NOT FormatMessageW and NOT
    '' strerror: those are locale-dependent, vary between Windows builds, and
    '' produce text no test could assert.
    public declare function FsErrorMessage alias "fb_UStrFsErrorMessage" _
        ( byval code as FsErrorCode ) as ustring

    '' Reads GetLastError( )/errno and maps it. Does not clear it. Takes no
    '' argument precisely so that no call site can pass a stale one.
    public declare function FsMapOsError alias "fb_FsMapOsError" ( ) as long

    '' The native code, unmapped, exactly as the OS reports it.
    public declare function FsOsError alias "fb_FsOsError" ( ) as long
end extern

'' The error itself. It OWNS its strings: an FsError outlives the call that
'' produced it -- that is the entire point of returning one -- so it cannot
'' borrow the caller's path. That is why it is a few hundred bytes rather than a
'' word, and why RFC-0004 returns the directory walk's element BYREF.
public type FsError
    '' Default-constructible because Result default-constructs both slots
    '' (std/outcome). A default FsError is not meaningful; it is FS_UNKNOWN.
    declare constructor( )
    declare constructor( byval c as FsErrorCode, byval osc as long, _
                         byref op as const ustring, byref p as const ustring )

    declare const function Code      ( ) as FsErrorCode
    declare const function OsCode    ( ) as long
    declare const function Path      ( ) as ustring
    declare const function Path2     ( ) as ustring
    declare const function Operation ( ) as ustring
    declare const function Message   ( ) as ustring

    declare sub SetPath2( byref p as const ustring )

private:
    as FsErrorCode m_code
    as long        m_oscode
    as ustring     m_op, m_path, m_path2
end type

constructor FsError( )
    this.m_code   = FS_UNKNOWN
    this.m_oscode = 0
end constructor

constructor FsError _
    ( _
        byval c as FsErrorCode, byval osc as long, _
        byref op as const ustring, byref p as const ustring _
    )

    this.m_code   = c
    this.m_oscode = osc
    this.m_op     = op
    this.m_path   = p
end constructor

const function FsError.Code( ) as FsErrorCode
    return this.m_code
end function

const function FsError.OsCode( ) as long
    return this.m_oscode
end function

const function FsError.Path( ) as ustring
    return this.m_path
end function

const function FsError.Path2( ) as ustring
    return this.m_path2
end function

const function FsError.Operation( ) as ustring
    return this.m_op
end function

const function FsError.Message( ) as ustring
    select case this.m_code
    case FS_TIMED_OUT        : return "timed out"
    case FS_CONNECTION_RESET : return "connection reset by peer"
    end select
    return FsErrorMessage( this.m_code )
end function

sub FsError.SetPath2( byref p as const ustring )
    this.m_path2 = p
end sub

'' ------------------------------------------------------- the result aliases
''
'' One per T the module returns. See the header comment for why this cannot be
'' a single generic alias.
public type FsResultBool  as Result( of boolean, FsError )
public type FsResultLong  as Result( of long,    FsError )
public type FsResultI64   as Result( of longint, FsError )
public type FsResultUStr  as Result( of ustring, FsError )
public type FsResultStr   as Result( of string,  FsError )

'' ------------------------------------------------------------ construction
''
'' DECLARED ON ONE LINE, DELIBERATELY. A '_' line continuation inside a GENERIC
'' PROCEDURE's parameter list corrupts the recorded parameter and the generic
'' then fails at INSTANTIATION with
''
''     error 59: Illegal specification, at parameter 2 (
''     ) of __FBGENPROC()
''
'' reported against result.bi rather than against the offending line. Measured
'' and reduced; it affects generic procedures only, and result.tiko never trips it
'' because Ok and Fail each already fit on one line. Until fbc is fixed, every
'' generic procedure in this module keeps its parameter list on one line.
''
'' AND THERE IS NO SEPARATE `declare` FOR EITHER. A generic procedure is defined
'' once, with no forward declaration -- adding one gives
''
''     error 147: Default types or suffixes are only valid in
''                -lang deprecated or fblite or qb, found 'T'
''
'' because the declaration form does not parse `( of T )`. result.tiko's Ok and
'' Fail are written the same way for the same reason.
public function FsFail( of T )( byval c as FsErrorCode, byref op as const ustring, byref p as const ustring ) as Result( of T, FsError )
    return Fail( of T, FsError )( FsError( c, FsOsError( ), op, p ) )
end function

'' The same, taking the code from the OS instead of from the caller.
public function FsFailOs( of T )( byref op as const ustring, byref p as const ustring ) as Result( of T, FsError )
    return Fail( of T, FsError )( FsError( cast( FsErrorCode, FsMapOsError( ) ), FsOsError( ), op, p ) )
end function

